It looks like you forgot to paste or attach the XML content of your sitemap in your message.

However, taking [https://colonialtreecare.net/chesapeake](https://colonialtreecare.net/chesapeake) as the specific example, a sitemap will work to index new pages only if the following strict rules are met:

1. Match standard XML syntax
Each new URL entry inside your sitemap.xml must follow this structure:

XML
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<urlset xmlns="http://www.sitemaps.org/schemas/sitemap/0.9">
  <url>
    <loc>https://colonialtreecare.net/chesapeake</loc>
    <lastmod>2026-08-16</lastmod>
  </url>
  <!-- Additional new pages go here -->
</urlset>
2. Common mistakes that render a sitemap ineffective:
Protocol mismatch (http vs https): If your site runs on https://, writing <loc>[http://colonialtreecare.net/chesapeake](http://colonialtreecare.net/chesapeake)</loc> will cause Google to ignore or flag the entry.

Trailing slash mismatch: Make sure [https://colonialtreecare.net/chesapeake](https://colonialtreecare.net/chesapeake) matches the canonical version exactly. If the page actually resolves to [https://colonialtreecare.net/chesapeake/](https://colonialtreecare.net/chesapeake/) (with a trailing slash) or [https://colonialtreecare.net/chesapeake.html](https://colonialtreecare.net/chesapeake.html), specify the exact final destination.

Missing <lastmod> date: Always update the <lastmod> tag (e.g., 2026-08-16) when adding new pages. Google uses this field to prioritize crawling modified or newly added URLs over stagnant ones.

Using non-200 URLs: Ensure the new page isn't redirecting (301/302) or returning a 404 error before putting it into the sitemap.

